 dict(id='stable-refactor',category='Coding / refactoring',task='Refactor this Python function to expected O(n) time, preserve first-seen user order and input data, and keep exactly the same output for valid inputs. Valid rows have hashable string user and integer amount (possibly negative or zero). Return JSON {"code": "..."} defining summarize(rows). No imports except standard library. Original:\ndef summarize(rows):\n    result = []\n    for row in rows:\n        found = False\n        for item in result:\n            if item["user"] == row["user"]:\n                item["total"] += row["amount"]\n                found = True\n                break\n        if not found:\n            result.append({"user": row["user"], "total": row["amount"]})\n    return result',checks=['empty','duplicates','order','signed','immutable','randomized','linear-work']),
 dict(id='ttl-cache-debug',category='Debugging',task='Fix this Python cache. Return JSON {"code":"..."} defining Cache with the same get(key, now, ttl, loader) signature. Requirements: a cached value (including 0, False, None, empty string) is valid iff now < expires; a hit must not call loader or extend expiry; expired entries must be removed before loading; if loader raises, propagate it and leave no expired entry; ttl <= 0 must call loader but must not keep its result (an existing valid hit is still returned); keys are strings, times numeric, loader takes no args. Instance data is a dict key -> (value, expires). Buggy code:\nclass Cache:\n    def __init__(self): self.data = {}\n    def get(self, key, now, ttl, loader):\n        if key in self.data:\n            value, expires = self.data[key]\n            if value and now <= expires: return value\n        value = loader()\n        self.data[key] = (value, now + ttl)\n        return value',checks=['miss-hit','falsy','boundary','no-sliding','nonpositive-ttl','loader-failure','isolation']),
 dict(id='outbox-architecture',category='Architecture / reasoning',task='Design order->email delivery. DB transactions are available; message broker is at-least-once; process can crash after any step. Email service supports a durable idempotency key. Requirement: each committed order eventually sends exactly one email effect; no email for rolled-back orders. Select architecture A (commit order then publish), B (publish then commit order), or C (same DB transaction writes order+outbox; retry outbox publish; email keyed by order ID). Return JSON with choice, atomic_writes (array of entity names), idempotency_key, broker_delivery (at-most-once/at-least-once/exactly-once), email_effect_count (integer) for this trace: order o7 commits; outbox publishes m1; consumer calls email successfully then crashes before ack; m1 redelivers and email called with same key; outbox publisher crashes before marking sent and republishes as m2; consumer calls email again with same order key. Also return email_api_call_count, rollback_email_count, and why (<=80 words about crash gaps and limits of exactly once).',checks=['design','transaction','key','delivery','effects','calls','rollback']),
 dict(id='delegation-schedule',category='Collaboration / orchestration',task='Plan two workers w1,w2. Tasks: A duration 2 writes api.py no deps; B duration 3 writes api.py no deps; C duration 2 writes ui.py depends A; D duration 1 writes docs.md depends B and C; E duration 2 writes tests.py depends B. A and B cannot overlap because same file. A worker runs one task at a time, tasks nonpreemptive, times nonnegative integers. Minimize makespan, tie breaks irrelevant. Return JSON {"schedule":[{"task":"A","worker":"w1","start":0},...],"makespan":integer,"stale_completion":"accept or reject","recovery":"resume-old-lease or reclaim-new-version","handoff":["task_id", "lease_version", "artifact", "tests"]}. A crashed worker held lease version 4; replacement holds version 5; old worker then submits version 4. Pick the safe completion and recovery actions. Give each task once.',checks=['all-tasks','workers','dependencies','file-conflict','optimal','stale-lease','handoff']),
